pd.merge_ordered()

is different from:

pd.merge()
pd.merge_ordered()

merge_ordered() is used to merge two DataFrames while keeping the result ordered, especially useful for time-series data.

===

Example
import pandas as pd

df1 = pd.DataFrame({
    "date": ["2024-01-01", "2024-01-03"],
    "sales": [100, 150]
})

df2 = pd.DataFrame({
    "date": ["2024-01-02", "2024-01-04"],
    "price": [50, 60]
})

Now:

result = pd.merge_ordered(df1, df2, on="date")

Result:

| date       | sales | price |
| ---------- | ----: | ----: |
| 2024-01-01 |   100 |   NaN |
| 2024-01-02 |   NaN |    50 |
| 2024-01-03 |   150 |   NaN |
| 2024-01-04 |   NaN |    60 |


Notice that the dates are automatically ordered.

=====

Simple difference

pd.merge()
     ↓
Merge DataFrames
     ↓
Common key

====

pd.merge_ordered()
     ↓
Merge DataFrames
     ↓
Common key
     ↓
Keep result ordered
     ↓
Especially useful for dates/time-series

====

Real-world example

For your appointment system, imagine:

Appointments
Date       Bookings
01-09      5
03-09      8

Revenue
Date       Revenue
02-09      ₹2000
04-09      ₹3000

pd.merge_ordered() can combine them into a chronologically ordered timeline.

======

merge() → normal DataFrame merging
merge_ordered() → merging + ordered result, particularly useful for time-series data



